1|Player-characters hear the holy man's prophecy. Refer to Table 16-i, entry 17-18, to learn the nature of the prophecy.
2-5|Player-characters hear the holy man trying to convert the crowd to some local deity. Roll [[1d100]] and 01-20 he is actually a sorcerer who is casting spells which deceive his listeners.
6-11|Player-characters hear the holy man, a shaman of the Bear totem, predict that the world ends in [[1d20]] days.
12-14|Holy man tries to persuade player-characters to go on a quest. Roll [[1d20]]: on a result of 1-15, the request is non-magical and can be ignored; on 16-20, the persuasion is a moderately-strong magical spell, and must be successfully resisted. See Table 29, the missions table, to determine particulars of the quest.
15-16|A holy man is preaching holy war. Roll [[1d20]]: on a result of 1-10, the crowd ignores him; on 11-19, the town watch or regular soldiers haul him off for inciting to riot; on 20, the crowd roars for blood, ready to follow the holy man anywhere.
17-19|A holy man is preaching a new religion, of Klystra the Goddess of Night. Roll [[1d20]]: on a result of 1-10, the crowd ignores him; on 11-18, the town watch or special temple guards are hauling him away for heresy; on 19, he is a con-man who is actually selling naughty pictures; on 20, a small band of his converts attack the player-characters as non-believers.
20|Depending on how the gamemaster feels, the holy man blesses or curses the player-characters. 20% chance the blessing or curse works.